<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Thanjavur</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Tanjore</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Thanjavur (Tanjore) is a city in the Kaveri delta of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. It was the capital of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola Empire</a> from the 9th century, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a> built the great Brihadisvara Temple, and later of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maratha-empire">Maratha</a> rulers of Thanjavur, who were notable patrons of music and painting. It passed under British control in 1799.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/thanjavur.jpg" alt="Thanjavur" width="320" height="320" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Arun2117dts 5 · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Thanjavur_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Thanjavur lies in the Cauvery delta, 340 km south-west of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chennai">Chennai</a> and 56 km east of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tiruchirappalli">Tiruchirappalli</a>. The tributaries of the Cauvery, among them the Grand Anaicut canal, the Vadavaaru and the Vennaaru, flow through it. The plains beside the river have been cultivated from time immemorial, but most of the city and its surroundings lie in the New Delta, a dry upland tract that was brought under irrigation in the early 19th century. The district is known as the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rice">rice</a> bowl of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamil-nadu">Tamil Nadu</a>. Several explanations of the name are given, among them a Tamil phrase meaning the town where refugees entered and a legend of an Asura named Tanjan, slain on the site by Neelamegha Perumal, an incarnation of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vishnu">Vishnu</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Sangam-period Tamil records do not mention Thanjavur, though some scholars think the city existed then. The region was conquered by the Mutharayars in the sixth century, who ruled it until 849. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> returned to prominence under Vijayalaya in about 850; he took Thanjavur from the Mutharayar king Elango Mutharayar and built a temple to the goddess Nisumbhasudani, and his son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aditya-i">Aditya I</a> consolidated the hold on the city. Thanjavur became the most important city of the Chola Empire and remained its capital until Gangaikonda Cholapuram emerged in about 1025. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rajaraja-i">Rajaraja I</a>, who reigned from 985 to 1014, built the Brihadeeswarar Temple there.</p><p>After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Cholas</a> the city was ruled by the Mutharayars, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pandya-dynasty">Pandyas</a>, the Vijayanagar Empire, the Madurai Nayaks, the Thanjavur Nayaks and the Thanjavur Marathas. The Bhonsle family ruled the Thanjavur region from 1674 to 1855, and most of the Maratha kingdom was annexed by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/british-empire">British Empire</a> in 1799. The Maratha palace, begun by the Nayaks, stayed in the family's hands. Thanjavur has been part of independent <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a> since 1947.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Thanjavur is an important centre of religion, art and architecture in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/india">India</a>. The Brihadeeswarar Temple, dedicated to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/shiva">Shiva</a> and covered inside with wall paintings of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chola-empire">Chola</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nayaka">Nayak</a> periods, became a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a> in 1987, and the Great Living Chola Temples around the city share that status. The historian Percy Brown called its construction a landmark in the evolution of building art in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/south-india">South India</a>. The city is also the home of Tanjore painting, a style unique to the region, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saraswati-mahal-library">Saraswati Mahal Library</a> dates from the end of the 16th century. Tamil is the widely spoken language, and Thanjavur Marathi and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kathiawar">Saurashtra</a> are also spoken.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Thanjavur” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Chola country lay in the flat plain of the Kaveri and its tributaries, now the districts of <b>Thanjavur</b>, Tiruchirapalli, Tiruvarur, Nagapattinam and others.</blockquote><a href="/empire/chola-empire">Chola Empire · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Around 1219 he invaded the Chola country, burned <b>Thanjavur</b> and Uraiyur and celebrated a victory coronation in the Chola heartland, forcing Rajaraja III to accept him as overlord; the Chola king was restored only through Hoysala intervention.</blockquote><a href="/empire/later-pandyan-empire">Later Pandyan Empire · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote>The Battle of Sripurambiyam, around 885, crushed Pandya power but left Aparajita dependent on his allies, among them the rising Chola dynasty of <b>Thanjavur</b>, which had grown up as an ally and subordinate of the Pallavas.</blockquote><a href="/empire/pallava-dynasty">Pallava dynasty · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Politically, the nayaka kingdoms that succeeded it, including Madurai, <b>Thanjavur</b>, Gingee, Ikkeri and the Wodeyars of Mysore, inherited its institutions and claimed its legitimacy.</blockquote><a href="/empire/vijayanagara-empire">Vijayanagara Empire · Global &amp; Regional Impact</a></li><li><blockquote>Royal patronage made it the religion of most Indian kingdoms, from the Guptas to the Cholas of the south, whose great temple at <b>Thanjavur</b> was completed around 1010.</blockquote><a href="/religion/hinduism">Hinduism · Spread</a></li><li><blockquote>Dialects grew from different sound changes: the classical iṅku, “here”, is iṅkū in the Kongu dialect of Coimbatore, inga in <b>Thanjavur</b> and iṅkai in parts of Sri Lanka.</blockquote><a href="/language/tamil">Tamil · Change &amp; Descendants</a></li></ul></section>
